      <p>HVAC is one of the clearest cases for Google Ads: when a system fails, people search, call and book within hours. It is also one of the easiest accounts to run badly, because three very different kinds of work arrive through the same few words.</p>
      <p>An emergency repair at night, a full system replacement and an annual tune-up are different searches, with different urgency, different job values and different conversion rates. An account that treats them the same pays replacement prices for tune-up searches and loses the emergency calls when they matter most.</p>

      <h2>Split the work the way customers search</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Emergency repair:</strong> urgent searches such as "AC not working" or "furnace repair near me". High intent, often at awkward hours, won by being visible and answering the phone</li>
        <li><strong>Replacement and installation:</strong> the highest-value jobs, with a longer decision, more comparison and often questions about financing. Worth their own campaign, ads and landing pages</li>
        <li><strong>Maintenance and tune-ups:</strong> lower value per job, but steady, plannable work and the start of a long customer relationship</li>
        <li><strong>Commercial HVAC</strong>, where you offer it, as its own campaign, because the buyer, the search terms and the contract value are all different</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Each gets its own budget, bids and ad schedule. Replacements can justify a much higher cost per lead than tune-ups, and the account should know that.</p>

      <h2>Local Services Ads and Search together</h2>
      <p>Where Local Services Ads are available for your area, they sit above everything else and charge per lead rather than per click. They suit urgent repair calls well. Search ads give you control over which jobs you appear for and what the ad says, which matters most for replacements.</p>
      <p>Run together and measured separately, each covers the gaps in the other. The <a href="/learn/local-services-ads-vs-google-ads/">Local Services Ads vs Google Ads</a> guide sets out the difference in detail.</p>

      <h2>Seasonality: plan for the first hot and cold days</h2>
      <p>HVAC demand does not rise gently. The first heatwave and the first cold snap bring a surge of repair searches in a matter of days. Accounts with a flat daily budget often run out by midday on exactly the days that matter most.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Budgets that can rise quickly when the weather turns</li>
        <li>Emergency campaigns scheduled for the hours your phones are answered</li>
        <li>Shoulder seasons used for maintenance plans and tune-up offers</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Measure booked jobs, not calls</h2>
      <p>The default Google Ads setup counts a call over a set length as a conversion. Suppliers, job seekers and people outside your area all count. Bidding then learns to buy more of whatever makes the phone ring.</p>
      <p>We connect conversions to the work that actually happens: calls and forms qualified against your booking or field service software, and booked jobs fed back to the campaign that produced them, with their value. Then cost per booked job, not cost per call, decides where the budget goes. <a href="/learn/why-google-ads-calls-are-not-becoming-jobs/">Why your Google Ads calls are not becoming jobs</a> explains the usual gaps.</p>

      <h2>Where HVAC budgets leak</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Job and training searches: technician jobs, HVAC school, certification courses</li>
        <li>DIY and parts searches from homeowners fixing the system themselves</li>
        <li>Searches from outside the service area, often because of the default location setting. See <a href="/learn/google-ads-location-targeting-settings/">the location setting that wastes budget</a></li>
        <li>Calls arriving when nobody can answer them</li>
      </ul>

